00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
hello and welcome to the daily climate  show coming up  2021's weather was unremarkable says the  met office but only because we're  getting used to a rapidly changing  planet  a u-turn in washington could mean  important progress for president biden's  climate agenda  and as gas prices soar and concerns over  supply continue how should the uk be  looking to secure its energy this winter  [Music]  welcome to the daily climate show where  we track the changes happening to our  world hear from those affected and  report on the race for solutions now sea  levels are rising more quickly and  warmer temperatures are the new normal  for britain that's according to an  annual review of last year's weather  from the met office although the report  says there was nothing remarkable about  the weather overall in 2021 this is all  in the context of climate change the  report goes on to explain that's because  our perception of what's normal is  changing  the report says temperatures last year  which should now be regarded as normal  would have been near record-breaking  just over 30 years ago  all of the uk's top 10 warmest years  have occurred this century since records  began back in 1884.  the met office says the rate of sea  level rise is increasing by around 16.5  centimeters since 1990.  it also suggests spring is coming  earlier raising concerns that plant and  animal life are not evolving quickly  enough to adapt sky's katarina vitovsky  has the story  today's met office report looks at last  year's weather but more crucially its  impact  2021 was another year in a pattern of  warming  that's changing things we take for  granted  spring is getting hotter and wetter and  the seasons are shifting now the warmer  weather has really sped up fruit  ripening and and nuts for me now what  that means is it sounds well what's the  problem with that but actually that  happening earlier can have a really  disastrous effect for wildlife because  they may not have the food source  available later in autumn when they need  it so there's real jeopardy for some  species here yeah there's real jeopardy  not we're in massive species decline  already  [Applause]  what's interesting about looking at last  year's weather is in some respects how  unremarkable it actually was in 2021  there were no extreme record highs but  why that is interesting is that if you  take that 12 months and look at it in a  broader context even without a record  high it was still one of the hottest  years on record  look at this at how much warmer 2021 was  and the average hottest day of the year  just a few decades ago  and remember today's report doesn't look  at this year 2022 that has already  changed the game entirely with the uk  average temperature exceeding 30 degrees  celsius for the first time ever  as climates change extremes become more  frequent last year saw storm irwin  batter parts of the uk with high winds  and snow eight months later just last  week it was that blistering heat  our natural world can adapt to gradual  change but shocks like extreme weather  in addition to our warming climate could  push some species too far to a point  beyond which they cannot recover  caterina over topsy sky news  well as you heard in katarina's report  the average temperatures in the uk are  rising every year and we're getting less  rain this month is not yet over but so  far it's been the driest july in england  since 1911 and the second driest since  records began in 1836.  figures from the met office show that  only 15.8 millimeters of rain fell on  average up to the 26th of july that's  just 24 of the amount which would be  expected in an average july  experts are warning that if the dry  weather continues it means there will be  a risk of drought with people  potentially being asked to reduce their  water consumption  well to the u.s now and after more than  a year of negotiations a u.s democratic  senator has stunned washington by  announcing a sudden support for  president joe biden's top agenda item a  climate bill senator joe manchin made a  joint statement with senate majority  leader chuck schumer saying they're come  they've come to an agreement on new  legislation addressing health care and  climate change since last year mr  manchin has been using his pivotal vote  in the 50-50 split senate to force  president biden and the democrats to  abandon a more ambitious version of the  bill well joe pike joins me now from  washington dc  joe this is really significant and great  news for the climate  yes and it was chloe it's fair to say a  shock for some a surprise for others and  for climate activists in this country  who've been pretty despondent in recent  months it changes everything it is a big  reset in joe biden's climate  agenda and his ambitions his aims to  reduce greenhouse gas emissions 50 below  2005 at levels by the end of this decade  those aims those targets look almost  achievable with this new bill why well  because we're talking about billions of  dollars of tax incentives incentives to  ramp up the production of wind solar and  geothermal energy incentives to keep  nuclear power stations open but also  incentives for lower income americans to  switch to electric vehicles so why was  it a surprise well as you say it's all  due to uh one man joe manchin this  democrat  senator from west virginia who has been  proving to be a bit of a nightmare for  joe biden a hold out and in this very  finely balanced senate he has refused  to back this until now that of course  has changed it is good news for joe  biden and it also means the u.s can keep  the pressure on other countries like  china and africa to keep up with their  climate change commitments  joe pike in washington for us thanks for  that  now let's get a bit more of the day's  climate news now sections of the world's  largest tropical peatland are being  auctioned today in the democratic  republic of congo  30 oil and gas blocks in the congo basin  which play a crucial role in africa's  climate system are going under the  hammer the move is angering  environmentalists who say oil drilling  poses a significant risk to the  continent already being affected by  climate change  seven people have been killed by  lightning in a village in india's  northern uttar pradesh state it brings  the total death toll by lightning  strikes to 49 people this week indian  officials say lightning has killed  nearly 750 people across the country  since april climate scientists believe  global warming has played a role in the  rising number of lightning strikes with  research suggesting a one degree celsius  rise in temperature increases the  frequency of lightning by 12  a 3.1 million pound tree planting  package has been announced in london it  comes after record-breaking temperatures  and wildfires which destroyed homes and  land in the capital mayor sadiq khan  says last week's events have laid bare  how vulnerable london is to the effects  of climate change  now the national grid has warned that  concerns over gas supplies from russia  could mean periods of uncertainty over  electricity supply this winter although  it says it expects to be able to meet  demand there are renewed calls for the  uk's energy network to reduce its  reliance on gas and shift its focus to  renewable energy  well right now just over half of our  electricity grid is supplied by  renewables that's solar wind and  hydropower while nearly half is gas  and while millions struggle with the  rising cost of living energy giants have  once again been reporting bumper profits  the parent company of british gas  centrica saw half-year operating profits  rise from 262 million pounds to 1.3  billion  meanwhile shell recorded earnings of  around 9.5 billion pounds for the three  months to the end of june more than  double the 4.5 billion pounds for the  same period last year well the firm's  chief executive has been defending the  figures and says shell is now investing  more money into its decarbonisation  strategy  we need to collectively as a society  spend a whole lot more on the transition  and we need to work much harder to  decarbonize the demand for uh  for energy as well and that is exactly  our strategy decarbonizing the demand  for for energy the spending on it steve  we're spending about this year a third  of our combined opex and capex on the  energy transition and we spend about 55  billion dollars a third goes to the  energy transition and if you look at  2025 it will be half so we are  definitely stepping up the the  investments there  well let's talk more about this now  we're joined by jess ralston senior  analyst at the climate and intelligence  unit jess thanks so much for joining us  today we just heard from shell's chief  executive there talking about spending  more money on decarbonisation but still  oil and gas companies are spending more  money on fossil fuels than they are  renewables  hi there yes that's absolutely right so  the fossil fuel industry currently has  plans to build 195 projects that would  each result in at least a billion tons  of co2 over their lifetimes so while  companies like shell and centrica are  investing in renewables there's no doubt  about that their green investments are  simply dwarfed by their fossil fuel  investments  and we have seen a windfall tax  introduced by mr sunap but of course  that's not a permanent solution it's  going to be a really tricky thing for  the next prime minister to navigate  yeah the reality is the oil and gas  companies are making huge amounts of  profit from higher gas prices while the  public could see their energy bills  reach nearly four grand this coming year  so it's easy to understand why the  public think that a windfall tax is a  fair approach but it will be interesting  to see how any prime minister deals with  calls from within their party to scrap  it i mean getting rid of it so quickly  after announcing it does create  uncertainty and you know investors won't  know if they're coming or going so a  longer term solution might be changing  the tax system altogether to make it  less lucrative in the first place for  these companies  and we're all watching the energy crisis  unfold in europe and no doubt wondering  what it means for supplies in the uk  how do you see things panning out here  well like you mentioned earlier national  grid expects to be able to meet our  demand albeit with some periods where  electricity margins are a little bit  tighter because of the uncertainty with  russia  but what we have in the uk is actually a  price problem rather than a security of  supply one like the eu has um you know  we're much less reliant on russian gas  and oil and but that being said although  we're an island we're very tied to what  happens in europe because we're  interconnected with their gas and  electricity markets through our  pipelines so what happens over there to  their prices also happens to ours and  we've seen our gas bills are probably  going to be pushed up by about two grand  by the expensive price of gas and so  until we went ourselves off it for good  we're almost certainly going to feel the  pinch from the volatility in the gas  markets  and while you mentioned it's more of a  price problem than a security problem  the uk still does need to secure its  supply  what do you see happening in the short  to medium term sort of more a bigger  push for renewables  well i think insulating our leaky homes  is probably the most sensible place to  start i mean reducing our energy demand  so we don't have to find and pay for as  much gas is very sensible and it would  help keep us warm this winter and people  are actually really keen to make their  homes more energy efficient you know  they want to save money on bills but  they also want to help with stopping  funding putin's war and in the medium to  longer term i think building out more  homegrown renewables so we need to  import less gas shields us from people  like putin turning on and off the gas  taps when they like to  jess ralston senior analyst at the  climate and intelligence unit thanks so  much for your thoughts on this tricky  subject  now you can get more on this story and  the rest of the week's climate news on  the climate show with tom heap this week  tom will be taking a look at how we need  to adapt our gardening habits to the  changing climate and tom can put your  questions to an expert just scan the qr  code on your screen right now to submit  a question and we'll do our very best to  give you an answer on the climate show  with tom heap on saturday and sunday at  3 30 and 7 30 p.m thanks very much for  joining us today that is all for now

